      "summary": "Riga: The Desert Sanctuary - Cities of Wonder: 40 Most Beautiful Cities in the World, Volume 5",
      "content": "### Chapter 2: Riga: The Desert Sanctuary\n\n![Riga - Iconic historic citadel view](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-05/images/riga-character.jpg)\n\nSituated on the wide Daugava River near its outlet into the Gulf of Riga, Latvia's capital was founded in 1201 by Bishop Albert and developed into the greatest trading metropolis of the Baltic Hanseatic League. The historic Old Town (Vecrīga) is a labyrinth of red-tiled roofs, medieval guild halls like the House of the Black Heads with its intricate Dutch Renaissance facade, and soaring brick spires including Saint Peter's Church.\n\n![Geographic location of Riga in Latvia](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-05/images/riga-map.png)\n\nBeyond the medieval core, Riga boasts the world's finest collection of Art Nouveau (Jugendstil) architecture, designed by Mikhail Eisenstein and local Latvian architects in the early twentieth century. Streets like Alberta Iela and Elizabetes Iela feature dazzling limestone facades adorned with screaming gargoyles, mythological sphinxes, sculpted peacocks, and sinuous floral curves, creating an open-air museum of Belle Époque architectural splendor."
