      "summary": "Toruń: The Desert Sanctuary - Cities of Wonder: 40 Most Beautiful Cities in the World, Volume 5",
      "content": "### Chapter 5: Toruń: The Desert Sanctuary\n\n![Toruń - Iconic historic citadel view](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-05/images/torun-character.jpg)\n\nStraddling the broad Vistula River in northern Poland, Toruń was founded by the Teutonic Order in the thirteenth century and developed into an autonomous Hanseatic trading city. Spared from destruction during modern European wars, the Old Town preserves one of the finest ensembles of intact Brick Gothic architecture in Central Europe, completely built of warm-toned red terracotta and baked clay.\n\n![Geographic location of Toruń in Poland](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-05/images/torun-map.png)\n\nThe monumental Old Town Hall in the central marketplace is among the largest Gothic civic buildings in Europe, neighboring the fourteenth-century cathedral and the leaning watchtower. Toruń is world-renowned as the birthplace of the astronomer Nicolaus Copernicus, who famously 'stopped the sun and moved the earth', as well as for its centuries-old tradition of baking spiced Toruń gingerbread (pierniki) in intricately carved wooden pear-wood molds."
