      "content": "### Chapter 21: Lübeck: The Desert Sanctuary\n\n![Lübeck - Iconic historic citadel view](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-05/images/lubeck-character.jpg)\n\nEncircled by the Trave River and historic moat canals, Lübeck was the supreme capital and 'Queen of the Hanseatic League'. The city entrance is marked by the monumental fifteenth-century Holstentor (Holsten Gate), an imposing Brick Gothic defense gate featuring twin leaning round towers, dark glazed brick friezes, and the proud Latin inscription 'Concordia domi foris pax' (Harmony within, peace without).\n\n![Geographic location of Lübeck in Germany](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-05/images/lubeck-map.png)\n\nThe old town island is famous for its 'Seven Spires', including Saint Mary's Church, the model for Brick Gothic cathedrals across the Baltic, whose soaring brick vaulting rises nearly forty meters high. Narrow passageways lead to secluded residential courtyards (Gänge) built for harbor workers, while the historic Café Niederegger opposite the Town Hall celebrates Lübeck's world-famous almond marzipan tradition."
